Pins:
```python
PIN_KEYS=("artifact_sha256","reviewed_sha256","sha256","cited_sha256","target_sha256","ledger_sha256","source_sha256","f0_sha256","declared_f0_sha256")
def collect_pins(d):
    ex=set(); tgt=set()
    def walk(obj, sink):
        if isinstance(obj,str):
            for m in re.findall(r'[0-9a-fA-F]{8,64}', obj):
                sink.add(m.lower())
        elif isinstance(obj,dict):
            for v in obj.values(): walk(v,sink)
        elif isinstance(obj,list):
            for v in obj: walk(v,sink)
    for k in PIN_KEYS:
        if k in d: walk(d[k], ex)
    for k in ("target","artifact"):
        if k in d: walk(d[k], tgt)
    return sorted(ex), sorted(tgt)
```
Note: for explicit pins, walk() extracts hex tokens from strings (including within dicts) — faithful enough to controller's `_explicit_pins` (which only takes direct str values of specific keys and target/artifact dict sha keys). For the controller reproduction I use the imported function, so no worry.

